<!--
    Objective-C ARC bookkeeping (`option objcarc on|off`).

    Issue: an ARC-compiled method reads as retain/release traffic around the few
    lines that do the work, and with the runtime entry points untyped the value
    an ARC helper hands on is lost. `-[Holder total:]` of the fixture (clang
    -fobjc-arc -O2) printed `objc_retain(arg1); ... objc_msgSend(arg1,sel_inner);
    v2 = objc_retainAutoreleasedReturnValue(); objc_release(arg1); ...
    objc_release(v2);` - the autoreleased return value arrived in a call with no
    argument and an `unsigned long` result. On the Toyota iOS app every method
    carries the same pattern through clang's outlined `objc_retain_x19()` /
    `objc_release_x20()`, shown with no argument at all.

    Fix: `p2_lift/kuna_objcarc.rs` registers `<callfixup>` payloads (the msvcftol
    mechanism) for the runtime's pass-through helpers (an incidental copy), for
    `objc_release` (no effect), for `objc_storeStrong` (the store) and for the
    outlined `objc_retain_x<N>`/`objc_release_x<N>`; the call-fixup installer
    tags a Mach-O's import stubs by address. `analyzers/objc/kuna_objcruntime.rs`
    gives every imported runtime entry point its header prototype, so with the
    calls shown they carry their argument and result.

    Pass 1 (default, `objcarc on`): the three methods are the work alone -
    `v1 = objc_msgSend(arg1,sel_count); v2 = objc_msgSend(arg1,sel_inner);
    return objc_msgSend(v2,sel_count) + v1;`, `self->slot = (Base *)arg1;`.
    Pass 2 (`objcarc off`): the calls are shown, typed -
    `obj = objc_retainAutoreleasedReturnValue((objc_object *)objc_msgSend(arg1,sel_inner));`.
-->
<bytechunk space="ram" offset="0x100000" readonly="true">
c0035fd6
</bytechunk>
<symbol space="ram" offset="0x100000" name="harness_stub"/>
</binaryimage>
